= Octave bindings =

libsigrok includes bindings for [[https://www.gnu.org/software/octave/ GNU Octave]], generated by [[https://www.swig.org/ SWIG]]. They give Octave scripts direct access to all sigrok hardware drivers, input/output formats and session management — no subprocess or file I/O needed.

== Requirements ==

* libsigrokcxx >= 0.4.0 (the libsigrok C++ bindings)
* SWIG >= 3.0.0
* GNU Octave >= 6.0, including development files (the <code>mkoctfile</code> tool)

On Debian/Ubuntu:
 sudo apt install octave liboctave-dev swig

On Fedora:
 sudo dnf install octave octave-devel swig

== Building ==

The Octave bindings are built as part of the normal libsigrok build:

 ./autogen.sh        # only needed when building from git
 ./configure
 make
 sudo make install

If any requirement is missing, ./configure will disable the Octave bindings automatically and the rest of libsigrok builds normally. You can also disable them explicitly:
 ./configure --disable-octave

== Installation ==

<code>make install</code> places the files into Octave's site-packages directories, queried from <code>mkoctfile -p LOCALOCTFILEDIR</code> and <code>mkoctfile -p LOCALFCNFILEDIR</code>. These directories are on Octave's load path by default, so no <code>addpath</code> call is required.

Installed files:
* <code>sigrok.oct</code> — the SWIG extension module (arch-specific oct directory)
* <code>sigrok_capture.m</code> — high-level capture helper (m-file directory)
* <code>sigrok_input.m</code> — high-level input-format helper (m-file directory)

== Usage ==

Load the bindings once at the start of your script or session:

 sigrok

This registers all wrapped classes (<code>Context</code>, <code>Driver</code>, <code>HardwareDevice</code>, etc.) in the Octave workspace.

=== Create a context ===

 sigrok
 ctx = Context.create();
 fprintf('libsigrok %s\n', ctx.lib_version);

=== List available drivers ===

<code>driver_list()</code> returns an Nx2 cell array <code>{name, Driver; ...}</code>:

 drivers = ctx.driver_list();
 for i = 1:size(drivers, 1)
     fprintf('%-30s  %s\n', drivers{i,1}, drivers{i,2}.long_name);
 end

=== Scan for devices ===

 drivers = ctx.driver_list();
 demo_idx = strcmp(drivers(:,1), 'demo');
 demo_drv = drivers{demo_idx, 2};

 devices = demo_drv.scan();   % returns cell array of HardwareDevice
 dev = devices{1};
 fprintf('%s %s %s\n', dev.vendor, dev.model, dev.version);

Pass driver options as a struct:
 opts.conn = 'tcp-raw/192.168.1.10/5555';
 devices = drv.scan(opts);

=== Inspect channels ===

 chs = dev.get_channels();    % cell array of Channel objects
 for i = 1:numel(chs)
     ch = chs{i};
     fprintf('[%d] %s  enabled=%d\n', ch.index, ch.name, ch.enabled);
 end

=== Capture data ===

Use the <code>sigrok_capture()</code> helper for a simple blocking capture:

 dev.open();
 opts.limit_samples = uint64(1000);
 [logic, analog] = sigrok_capture(ctx, dev, opts);
 dev.close();

 % logic: cell array of uint8 matrices (samples x unit_size)
 % analog: cell array of double matrices (channels x samples)

 data = vertcat(analog{:});          % channels x total_samples
 plot(data(1,:));                    % plot first analog channel

Extract a logic channel (0-based index):
 raw = vertcat(logic{:});            % N x unit_size uint8 matrix
 ch0 = bitand(raw(:,1), uint8(1)) > 0;   % channel 0 as logical vector

=== Configure a device ===

 dev.open();
 ck_sr  = ConfigKey.get_by_identifier('samplerate');
 ck_lim = ConfigKey.get_by_identifier('limit_samples');

 % Check what sample rates are available
 rates = dev.config_list(ck_sr);    % returns vector of uint64

 % Set sample rate and sample count
 dev.config_set(ck_sr,  uint64(1000000));
 dev.config_set(ck_lim, uint64(10000));

=== Read an input file ===

Use the <code>sigrok_input()</code> helper:

 ifmts = ctx.input_format_list();   % Nx2 cell {name, InputFormat; ...}
 vcd_idx = strcmp(ifmts(:,1), 'vcd');
 vcd_fmt = ifmts{vcd_idx, 2};

 raw = uint8(fileread('capture.vcd'));   % read file as bytes
 inp = vcd_fmt.create_input();
 [logic, analog] = sigrok_input(ctx, inp, raw);

=== Write an output file ===

 ofmts = ctx.output_format_list();
 vcd_idx = strcmp(ofmts(:,1), 'vcd');
 vcd_fmt = ofmts{vcd_idx, 2};

 udev = ctx.create_user_device('Test', 'MyDevice', '1.0');
 udev.add_channel(uint32(0), ChannelType.LOGIC, 'CLK');
 udev.add_channel(uint32(1), ChannelType.LOGIC, 'DATA');

 out = vcd_fmt.create_output(udev);

 hdr  = ctx.create_header_packet();
 meta_cfg.samplerate = uint64(1000000);
 meta = ctx.create_meta_packet(meta_cfg);
 raw  = uint8([0; 1; 2; 3]);        % 4 samples, 1 byte/sample
 lgc  = ctx.create_logic_packet(raw, uint64(1));
 endp = ctx.create_end_packet();

 vcd_text = [out.receive(hdr), out.receive(meta), ...
             out.receive(lgc), out.receive(endp)];
 fid = fopen('out.vcd', 'w');
 fprintf(fid, '%s', vcd_text);
 fclose(fid);

=== Session with datafeed callback ===

For full control, use a session directly:

 sess = ctx.create_session();
 dev.open();
 sess.add_device(dev);

 packets = {};
 sess.add_datafeed_callback(@(device, pkt) my_cb(device, pkt));
 sess.start();
 sess.run();     % blocks until session ends

 function my_cb(device, pkt)
     if strcmp(pkt.type.name, 'ANALOG')
         pl = pkt.payload_analog();
         data = pl.data();   % channels x samples double matrix
     elseif strcmp(pkt.type.name, 'LOGIC')
         pl = pkt.payload_logic();
         raw = pl.data();    % samples x unit_size uint8 matrix
     elseif strcmp(pkt.type.name, 'END')
         % session finished
     end
 end

== API naming differences from C++ ==

The Octave binding follows the C++ API closely, with a few differences forced by Octave's syntax rules:

{| class="wikitable"
! C++ API !! Octave binding !! Reason
|-
| <code>context.drivers</code> (property) || <code>ctx.driver_list()</code> || Returns Nx2 cell instead of map
|-
| <code>context.input_formats</code> || <code>ctx.input_format_list()</code> || Same
|-
| <code>context.output_formats</code> || <code>ctx.output_format_list()</code> || Same
|-
| <code>driver.scan(map)</code> || <code>drv.scan()</code> / <code>drv.scan(struct)</code> || Accepts Octave struct
|-
| <code>input_format.create_input(map)</code> || <code>fmt.create_input()</code> / <code>fmt.create_input(struct)</code> || Same
|-
| <code>output_format.create_output(dev, map)</code> || <code>fmt.create_output(dev)</code> / <code>fmt.create_output(dev, struct)</code> || Same
|-
| <code>input.send(data)</code> || <code>input.send_data(uint8_vec)</code> || <code>send</code> conflicts; accepts uint8 vector
|-
| <code>input.end()</code> || <code>input.input_end()</code> || <code>end</code> is a reserved keyword in Octave
|-
| <code>input.device</code> (property) || <code>input.get_device()</code> || Attribute renamed by shared SWIG layer
|-
| <code>device.channels</code> (property) || <code>dev.get_channels()</code> || Returns cell array; works on all device types
|-
| <code>channel_group.channels</code> || <code>cg.get_channels()</code> || Same
|-
| <code>session.trigger</code> (getter) || <code>sess.get_trigger()</code> || Paired with set_trigger()
|-
| <code>session.set_trigger(t)</code> || <code>sess.set_trigger(t)</code> || Accepts wrapped Trigger object
|}

